{
  "packs": [
    "core",
    "basics",
    "github-actions",
    {
      "id": "grow_with_claudinite",
      "config": {
        "pack_paths": [
          ".claudinite/local/packs",
          "packs"
        ]
      }
    },
    "tidy-repo",
    {
      "id": "local/claudinite",
      "config": {
        "canaryRepo": "missingbulb/ClaudiniteCanary"
      }
    },
    "local/canon-curation",
    {
      "id": "barriers",
      "answers": {
        "goals": "Keep extending.md's engine/content boundary structural: core (checks/, mount/, engine/, .github/, the top-level docs, and the shared engine modules under packs/ and skills/) must never name a specific pack or skill — packs and skills are discovered content, and a core file citing one couples the engine to its content. One rule per namespace (packs/*, skills/*); reviewed inherent couplings live in each rule's except. migrations/ is deliberately NOT core here: a migration record is a dated one-off that exists to move the fleet off a specific pack's old shape, so naming that pack is its purpose, not a coupling — and records accumulate as dated folders, one per change, which no path-keyed exception list should have to chase."
      },
      "config": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "from": [
              "engine",
              ".github",
              "bootstrap.md",
              "consumer-safe-changes.md",
              "extending.md"
            ],
            "to": "packs/*",
            "matchNames": true,
            "matchUniqueFilenames": false,
            "reason": "core is the engine that runs pack content — it must not name the specific packs it runs (extending.md)",
            "except": [
              {
                "path": ".github/workflows/README.md",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension",
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): the core .github/ README documents the chrome-extension-release plumbing being vendored out of core (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": ".github/workflows/chrome-extension-daily-release.yml",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension",
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release",
                  "packs/github-actions"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): chrome-extension-release reusable release infra hosted in the core .github/ tree; being vendored out (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": ".github/workflows/chrome-extension-publish-store.yml",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension",
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): chrome-extension-release reusable release infra hosted in the core .github/ tree; being vendored out (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": ".github/workflows/chrome-extension-release.yml",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension",
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): chrome-extension-release reusable release infra hosted in the core .github/ tree; being vendored out (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": ".github/actions/read-release-config/read-config.mjs",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): a chrome-release composite action in the core .github/ tree; being vendored out (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": "extending.md",
                "to": [
                  "packs/core"
                ],
                "reason": "inherent(#385): the engine map documents where the adoption-interview machinery lives now that it is bundled in the adoption skill (moved with that skill into the core pack, #835)"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "siblings": "packs",
            "to": "docs",
            "reason": "docs/ is outside the vendor set, so a pack file's reference to it resolves to nothing in every consumer (#424) — cite the doc from the issue or PR instead"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "claude-code-web-users-support",
      "config": {
        "repo": "missingbulb/Shepherd"
      },
      "answers": {
        "store": "missingbulb/Shepherd — the fleet's own repo, which holds one <email>.md per person under preferences/"
      }
    }
  ],
  "accept": [
    {
      "rule": "task-prework-env",
      "path": "packs/core/tasks/update/worker.mjs",
      "reason": "the update worker has a SECOND, deliberate caller: .github/workflows/canary-rehearsal.yml drives it directly to qualify a release ref, and CLAUDINITE_CANON_REF is that harness's own parameter, set there and read nowhere else. The rule's concern is a parameter channel that has silently stopped being delivered; this one is delivered by the workflow beside it, in the same repo, and its absence under prework is the normal case the worker already handles (rehearsalRef null = a real cycle)"
    },
    {
      "rule": "reference-integrity",
      "path": "docs/task-prework/",
      "reason": "the task-prework DESIGN is the dated record of migration #405 \u2014 it names the slot scheduler's entry point as the staging it was changing, and #974 has since deleted that module. The reference is dangling because the record is true of the moment it describes; repointing it at the queue's executor would make the document describe a design that never happened. Same standing as the per-project-scheduling entry below"
    },
    {
      "rule": "reference-integrity",
      "path": "docs/per-project-scheduling/",
      "reason": "the per-project-scheduling DESIGN/MIGRATION pair is the historical record of migration #394 — it names the central routine and planner files this migration deleted precisely because deleting them is what it records; rewriting those names would falsify the record, so its inbound-reference dangling is deliberate and permanent"
    },
    {
      "rule": "barrier",
      "path": ".github/workflows/canary-rehearsal.yml",
      "reason": "the live canary gate names packs/core/tasks/update because the worker is ITSELF vendored content, so a release that changes the worker cannot be rehearsed by the canary's vendored copy — that copy predates the release by definition, and a rehearsal driving it would ignore CLAUDINITE_CANON_REF entirely and run a real cycle against main, opening a PR on the canary instead of qualifying the ref. The crossing buys the one property the gate exists for: the code under test is the code every member runs the moment the release reaches them. The workflow names no pack CONTENT — only the entry point of the task that performs the converge"
    }
  ],
  "taskScheduler": {
    "endpoints": {
      "default": {
        "url": "[REDACTED:env:ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL]/v1/claude_code/routines/trig_01N7pUAHt3fs391BTqfN2bwM/fire",
        "tokenSecret": "CCR_ROUTINE_TOKEN"
      },
      "fleet": {
        "url": "[REDACTED:env:ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL]/v1/claude_code/routines/trig_0135i4BKGwoLbRGvKRiqqE4r/fire",
        "tokenSecret": "CCR_FLEET_ROUTINE_TOKEN"
      }
    },
    "dailyHour": 4,
    "weeklyDay": "Sun",
    "monthlyDay": 1
  }
}

// grow_with_claudinite task: growth-extract — the growth lifecycle's CAPTURE
// stage (per-project-scheduling DESIGN §6). ONE task over BOTH lesson sources:
// it runs the extract-from-activity skill over the window's commits/PRs/issues
// and the extract-from-conversations skill over the captured conversation logs,
// then runs prose-to-checks over what it just wrote to see whether any of it
// upgrades to a check — and lands the whole run through a single PR delivered
// per the repo's delivery settings (task.md → the shared deliver-pr.md
// procedure). Worker: task.md.
//
// The two halves were separate tasks (growth-extract + conversation-extract)
// firing at the same daily anchor, each opening its own PR against the same local
// packs on the same night. They shared the lesson bar, the promotion ladder, the
// dedup surface and the landing mechanics, so splitting them bought nothing and
// cost a second opus dispatch, a second PR, and two runs deduping against a
// corpus the other one was concurrently writing. One task, two source skills.
//
// Self-contained (imports nothing): the whole contract is this default export.

export default {
  id: 'growth-extract',
  frequency: 'daily-1h',           // the 03:00 anchor — lessons captured from an already-converged mount (DESIGN §2)
  // …and the ordering the anchor hour only IMPLIES, declared (tasks-dispatch
  // DESIGN §9): this task reads a mount `core/update` converges, so it yields while
  // that task's item is live this cycle and runs the moment it converges — or
  // rolls. The staggered anchor hours express the same intent and cannot enforce
  // it; this can.
  after: ['core/update'],
  precondition_signals: ['commits', 'prs', 'issues'],
  agent_model: 'opus',                   // generalizing/curating lessons is the heaviest judgment, and the default delivery lands the PR with no human review
  expected_outcome: 'merged-pr',            // additive edits to the repo's own local packs; delivered to land per the repo's delivery settings
  agent_instructions: 'task.md',
  agent_execution_timeout: 2700,            // two source passes plus the prose-to-checks upgrade — generous bound, extreme protection

  // ONE reason to run: a SUBSTANTIVE default-branch change. A bot bump / [skip ci] /
  // nightly-baselining commit advancing main is not a lesson to extract, so
  // `commits.substantiveChange` already applies that classification. It arms the
  // activity half AND means a fresh capture now sits on the logs branch, so the
  // conversation half runs too.
  //
  // There WAS a second arm — a log actually past retention, which existed only so
  // the retention prune still fired on a repo gone quiet. The prune is its own
  // agentless task now (../logs-prune/), so that arm left with it and this opus
  // dispatch no longer happens on nights with nothing to extract.
  //
  // The activity half is scoped by the substantive shas + touched PR/issue numbers
  // passed as binding scope — INCLUDING the PRs merged during the window, whose
  // review discussion is usually its richest lesson source. Context is binding
  // scope and task.md forbids widening past it, so a merged PR the precondition
  // does not name is unreadable to the worker.
  precondition(signals) {
    const commits = signals.commits ?? {};
    if (commits.substantiveChange !== true) {
      return { run: false, reason: 'no substantive default-branch change in the window — nothing to extract' };
    }

    const shas = (commits.list ?? []).filter((c) => c.substantive).map((c) => c.sha.slice(0, 7));
    const prs = signals.prs?.touched ?? [];
    const merged = (signals.prs?.merged ?? []).map((p) => p.number);
    const issues = signals.issues?.touched ?? [];

    const context = [`Activity half IS in scope: the ${shas.length} substantive commit(s) in the window — ${shas.join(', ')}.`];
    if (merged.length) context.push(`PRs merged in the window — read each one's diff and its review discussion: ${merged.map((n) => `#${n}`).join(', ')}.`);
    if (prs.length) context.push(`PRs touched in the window: ${prs.map((n) => `#${n}`).join(', ')}.`);
    if (issues.length) context.push(`Issues touched in the window: ${issues.map((n) => `#${n}`).join(', ')}.`);
    context.push('Conversation half IS in scope: a substantive merge means fresh captures on origin/conversation-logs — run the fresh pass over the recent window.');

    return { run: true, reason: `${shas.length} substantive commit(s) in the window`, context };
  },
};

